<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kittur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1585 CE – 1824 CE (approx.) · capital Kittur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Lingayat chiefdom in northern Karnataka whose queen, Rani Chennamma, led one of the earliest armed revolts against the East India Company.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kittur was founded in the late 16th century by a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> chiefs who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Adil Shahi</a> sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and held the title Desai. The territory lay between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belagavi">Belgaum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharwad">Dharwad</a> in the fertile black-soil country of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Desais ruled from the fort of Kittur, paying tribute successively to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and Mysore. Mallasarja, who died in 1816, was its best-known ruler. The state was predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>-speaking, with an economy based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and grain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Shivalingarudra Sarja died in 1824 the Company refused to recognise his adopted heir. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kittur-chennamma">Rani Chennamma</a> resisted, and her forces killed the collector St John Thackeray, but Kittur was captured in December 1824 and annexed. She is honoured today as a pioneer of Indian resistance.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1585 CE – 1824 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36"